[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Hibernate Session Methods: persist, merge, saveOrUpdate, update, delete, and refresh"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate Session management is a core concept for ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) in Java. Understanding the nuances between state-transition methods like persist, merge, saveOrUpdate, update, delete, and refresh is critical for avoiding LazyInitializationException, detached entity exceptions, and maintaining data integrity."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. persist(Object entity)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The persist method is used to make a transient instance persistent. It transitions the entity state from transient to persistent. JPA compliant, it does not guarantee an immediate SQL INSERT; instead, it defers execution until the flush or commit phase if generated identifiers are used."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = new User("Alice");
session.persist(user);

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. merge(Object entity)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The merge method copies the state of the given detached object onto the persistent entity with the same identifier. If there is no persistent instance currently associated with the session, it loads it. Merge returns a new persistent instance, leaving the original detached object untouched."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

// userDetached comes from an older session or external layer
User detachedUser = new User();
detachedUser.setId(1L);
detachedUser.setName("Bob Updated");

// merge returns the managed instance
User managedUser = (User) session.merge(detachedUser);

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. update(Object entity)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The update method reattaches a detached instance to the current session. If an instance with the same identifier is already persistent in the session, Hibernate throws a NonUniqueObjectException. It always issues an SQL UPDATE statement upon flushing, regardless of whether the entity fields actually changed."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = new User();
user.setId(2L);
user.setName("Charlie");

session.update(user); // Reattaches the detached entity

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. saveOrUpdate(Object entity)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"This method handles both transient and detached instances. If the entity is transient, it saves it (SQL INSERT). If it is detached, it updates it (SQL UPDATE). Hibernate determines the state by checking if the identifier is null or if the version property indicates a new instance."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = new User();
// If id is null, it inserts. If id exists, it updates.
user.setId(3L); 
user.setName("Diana");

session.saveOrUpdate(user);

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"5. delete(Object entity) / remove"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The delete method (or remove in JPA) transitions a persistent or detached entity state into a removed state. This schedules an SQL DELETE statement to be executed upon session flush or transaction commit."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = session.get(User.class, 1L);
if (user != null) {
    session.delete(user);
}

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"6. refresh(Object entity)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The refresh method reloads the state of the given persistent instance from the database, overwriting any changes made to the object in memory during the current session. This is useful when database triggers or concurrent transactions modify the data."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = session.get(User.class, 1L);
user.setName("Modified in memory");

// Discards local changes and reloads from DB
session.refresh(user);

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Best Practices"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use persist() for new transient entities when adhering strictly to JPA standards."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use merge() when working with detached objects where another managed instance might already be loaded in the session context."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid update() if you are unsure whether another instance with the same identifier is already loaded in the session to avoid NonUniqueObjectException."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use refresh() sparingly to sync stale application memory state with the underlying relational database."}]
